The patient’s symptoms—fever, flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness, blood in the urine, and pain with urination—are strongly suggestive of an upper urinary tract infection (likely pyelonephritis). In addition, her diabetes increases her risk of complications. In managing suspected pyelonephritis, it is important first to confirm the diagnosis and document the urinary infection by obtaining a urine analysis and urine culture. This step will guide appropriate empirical antibiotic therapy (which may be started empirically after obtaining the sample) and is more useful than immediately starting an antibiotic like ceftazidime (an agent often reserved for Pseudomonas infections) or getting an abdominal CT scan without further evidence of stones or abscess. 

Thus, the best next step in management is to obtain a urine analysis and urine culture.

