This patient’s facial swelling and orofacial fistula are highly suggestive of bisphosphonate‐associated osteonecrosis of the jaw. In multiple myeloma, bisphosphonates (or, more recently, denosumab) are frequently given to prevent skeletal complications. Bisphosphonates work by binding to hydroxyapatite in the bone matrix, which leads to the inhibition of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. This mechanism can result in impaired bone remodeling and, potentially, jaw osteonecrosis with subsequent infection or fistula formation.

Thus, the drug mechanism most likely responsible for this patient’s symptoms is:
  \boxed{C}

