A 4‐year‐old boy who “falls” repeatedly is often found to have an underlying neuromuscular weakness. Among the choices, Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is a common cause of progressive weakness in young boys due to the absence of the dystrophin protein. This defect leads to repeated muscle fiber damage and weakness, especially in the proximal muscles, which contributes to an unsteady gait and frequent falls. Although ataxia telangiectasia (loss of ATM protein) also causes falls through cerebellar ataxia, DMD is far more common and is classically associated with early signs such as a waddling gait, difficulty with stairs, and falls. The other options (SMN1 gene defect and arylsulfatase A deficiency) have different clinical pictures that are less typical in causing falls in a 4‐year‐old.

Therefore, the most likely underlying mechanism of the patient’s fall is the absence of the dystrophin protein.

