We can narrow the answer by remembering the asthma severity‐criteria used to classify asthma control. For an 8‐year‐old, the categories are defined by (1) frequency of symptoms (i.e. day‐time and nocturnal awakenings), (2) limitation of normal activities, (3) need for rescue medications, and (4) auscultatory findings. In this case the details provided to the pediatrician had indicated that the child experiences daily symptoms, is using her short‐acting beta–agonist more than once a day on several days per week, and has some limitation in her routine activities. These features point away from the “mild,” where symptoms are only every few days with little limitation (option A or D), and from “severe persistent” which would involve very frequent, almost continuous symptoms with multiple weekly exacerbations and extreme limitations (option B). Instead, the description fits a pattern of regular daily symptoms with some nighttime issues that does not reach the threshold for severe persistent asthma. That is most consistent with the “moderate persistent” category (option C). 

