We are faced with a few measures that can reduce the risk of getting an infectious condition. Although each option might help with food‐ or water‐borne diseases, only one is known to “nip in the bud” a range of severe, vaccine‐preventable infections that a young child without a past history might otherwise acquire. In practice, many life‐threatening childhood diseases (for example, those that can present with neurologic, respiratory, or septic complications such as diphtheria, tetanus, Haemophilus influenzae type b meningitis, or pertussis) are prevented by routine immunizations. Unlike measures such as “keeping all food stored in the refrigerator” (which is specific to food‐storage–related toxin/virus growth) or “avoiding undercooked seafood” (which addresses a particular problem only encountered in those who eat raw seafood), immunizations protect against a broader range of pathogens in a highly effective manner. Although “better hand‐washing practices” is important in reducing many communicable diseases, its effect is more general and does not prevent the serious invasive infections that are now rare because of vaccine programs.

Thus, if the child’s “new symptoms” were due to an infection that is well covered by routine vaccines, then appropriate immunization would have been the key public health intervention.

