The nearly “flat‐topped” curve when the patient drinks a concentrated glucose solution indicates that a very large proportion of the glucose is taken up very rapidly from the blood. In nonpregnant persons and in many tissues, insulin binds its receptor and triggers the translocation of GLUT4 into the plasma membrane of adipose (fat) cells (and muscle cells) so that they take up glucose. Pregnancy is typically associated with increasing insulin secretion (to overcome insulin resistance – especially in the second and third trimesters). However, in this case the blood glucose barely rises (from 91 to 94 mg/dL), which strongly suggests that the hormone (insulin) is acting very effectively to clear the blood glucose by moving it into insulin‐sensitive cells. Among the choices, adipocytes are well known to rapidly take up glucose via insulin‐stimulated GLUT4.

Thus, the hormone responsible (insulin) most likely increases the glucose transport into adipocytes.

Final Answer: \boxed{A}

