The lesion—a painless ulcer with a black eschar and surrounding edema—is characteristic of cutaneous anthrax, an infection caused by Bacillus anthracis. An important virulence factor of this organism is the edema factor, which is an adenylate cyclase. When inside host cells, edema factor converts ATP to cyclic AMP, raising intracellular cAMP levels and ultimately contributing to the edema observed at the infection site.

Thus, the mechanism of action of the edema factor resembles that of the adenylate cyclase enzyme.

Answer: \boxed{B}

The lesions are small, tension-filled, subepidermal bullae that occur on the extremities (arms) without mucosal involvement. This clinical picture is typical of bullous pemphigoid, an autoimmune blistering disorder where autoantibodies target proteins in the hemidesmosomes (such as BP180/BP230) that anchor the epidermis to the dermis. In contrast, antibodies against desmogleins (as in pemphigus vulgaris) target intercellular junctions between epidermal cells, leading to flaccid intraepidermal blisters that easily rupture (Nikolsky sign) and commonly involve mucosal surfaces. The other choices are not consistent with the described blistering pattern.

Thus, the patient’s condition is most likely due to autoimmunity directed against components of the hemidesmosomes.

