The patient's BMPR2 mutation and symptoms are highly suggestive of pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH). In the treatment of PAH, endothelin receptor antagonists (such as bosentan) are commonly used to delay disease progression. One of the significant concerns with bosentan is its potential to cause hepatotoxicity, so patients must have regular liver function monitoring. Although phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors (choice B) are also used to treat PAH, they typically do not require as intensive liver enzyme monitoring as endothelin receptor antagonists.

Thus, the medication described is an endothelin-1 receptor antagonist.

