The key to answering the question is recognizing which antihypertensive drug—and through which signaling pathway—causes smooth‐muscle relaxation in the renal arteries. In hypertensive emergencies, an agent that selectively dilates the renal vasculature is sometimes used to protect renal blood flow. Fenoldopam is a selective dopamine‐1 (D₁) receptor agonist that works on these receptors, which are linked to the Gs protein. Activation of the Gs protein stimulates adenylyl cyclase, leading to an increased production of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP). Elevated cAMP then activates protein kinase A (PKA), which ultimately leads to smooth‐muscle relaxation in the vascular wall. In this case, the “intracellular change” in the renal vascular smooth muscle is the rise in cyclic AMP.

Thus, the correct answer is:

