The patient's multiple myeloma causes production of abnormal immunoglobulin light chains. These light chains can deposit in tissues as amyloid, leading to AL (primary) amyloidosis, which frequently involves the kidneys. In kidney biopsies from patients with AL amyloidosis, Congo red staining reveals the characteristic apple-green birefringence. The other options (mutated transthyretin, calcitonin, and β-amyloid) are associated with familial amyloidosis, medullary carcinoma of the thyroid, and Alzheimer’s disease respectively, none of which are typically linked to multiple myeloma.

Thus, the most likely kidney biopsy finding in this patient is deposition of AL amyloid.

